5:The scope here is the geometric and mathematical development of non-Abelian Yang–Mills theory: principal bundles and connection forms; local gauge potentials and curvature; the classical Yang–Mills action; gauge fixing, Faddeev–Popov, BRST and BV; perturbative gauge-preserving renormalization; topological sectors and instantons; lattice observables and confinement; and the four-dimensional existence/mass-gap frontier. General EFT and anomaly-matching interfaces belong to the adjacent scaffold, while supersymmetry transformations, supermultiplets, BPS sectors, localization, and dualities belong to the supersymmetry scaffold. This note proposes a few shared gauge suppliers for those tracks but does not take ownership of their distinct results.
7:## Scope, assumptions, and authoring boundary
9:The authors should begin with an explicitly selected compact Lie group \(G\), including its global form, a finite-dimensional unitary representation \(R\) for every matter multiplet, a principal right \(G\)-bundle \(P\to M\), and the spacetime geometry. The global group is part of the physical model. A Lie algebra alone does not identify the global gauge group or its allowed representations: \(SU(N)\), \(PSU(N)\), and other central quotients can have the same Lie algebra and different line operators and charge lattices. Do not use a group label such as “\(SU(N)\) gauge theory” as shorthand for both the local Lie algebra and the chosen bundle topology.
13:For a chosen invariant positive-definite inner product on \(\mathfrak g\), specify the normalization of the action, trace, coupling, topological charge, and theta term. In four dimensions and natural units, the gauge field has engineering dimension one, its curvature dimension two, the coupling dimension zero, and the action density dimension four. These assignments are model conventions. The author must also give the quantity types, physical roles, units, suppressed constants, and scope required by PHYSICS-CONTENT-MODEL.md. No measured value, empirical claim, or continuum limit follows from a formal classical or regulated model.
