        "def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic": "mathematical-premise",
        "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus": "mathematical-premise"
      },
      "inventory_expansion_required": false,
      "statement": "Let $\\mathcal A=\\mathcal A_{\\bar0}\\oplus\\mathcal A_{\\bar1}$ be the finite-dimensional supercommutative complex coefficient algebra, and let $\\Lambda_4$ have ordered generators $\\theta^1,\\theta^2,\\bar\\theta^{\\dot1},\\bar\\theta^{\\dot2}$ and the product, parity, derivatives and conjugation specified in the four-dimensional superspace Grassmann algebra. Let $\\mathscr S=C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^{1,3},\\mathcal A\\otimes\\Lambda_4)$ be the space of smooth coefficient functions; $\\partial_\\mu$ differentiates the real coordinate $x^\\mu$ and acts trivially on the finite Grassmann basis. Use the mostly-plus metric and the matrices $\\sigma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\dot\\alpha}$ specified by the four-dimensional super-Poincare algebra. A **chiral superfield** is an even element $\\Phi\\in\\mathscr S$ satisfying the two equations\n\n$$\\bar D_{\\dot\\alpha}\\Phi=0,\\qquad \\bar D_{\\dot\\alpha}:=-\\partial^L_{\\bar\\theta^{\\dot\\alpha}}-i\\sum_{\\alpha,\\mu}\\theta^\\alpha\\sigma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\dot\\alpha}\\partial_\\mu,\\quad \\dot\\alpha\\in\\{\\dot1,\\dot2\\}.$$\n\nDefine the nilpotent coordinate shift $\\Delta y^\\mu=i\\sum_{\\alpha,\\dot\\alpha}\\theta^\\alpha\\sigma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\dot\\alpha}\\bar\\theta^{\\dot\\alpha}$ and $y^\\mu=x^\\mu+\\Delta y^\\mu$. For a smooth coefficient function $f$, $f(y)$ means the finite Taylor substitution $\\sum_{k=0}^{2}(\\Delta y^{\\mu_1}\\cdots\\Delta y^{\\mu_k}/k!)\\,\\partial_{\\mu_1}\\cdots\\partial_{\\mu_k}f(x)$, with the $k=0$ term equal to $f(x)$ and repeated spacetime indices summed; higher terms vanish in $\\Lambda_4$. The chiral components are the unique smooth coefficient functions $A,F\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^{1,3},\\mathcal A_{\\bar0})$ and $\\psi_1,\\psi_2\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^{1,3},\\mathcal A_{\\bar1})$ in\n\n$$\\Phi(y,\\theta)=A(y)+\\theta^1\\psi_1(y)+\\theta^2\\psi_2(y)+\\theta^1\\theta^2F(y).$$\n\nThe total parity of every term is even. Define the supersymmetry generators on $\\mathscr S$ by\n\n$$Q_\\alpha=\\partial^L_{\\theta^\\alpha}-i\\sum_{\\mu,\\dot\\alpha}\\sigma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\dot\\alpha}\\bar\\theta^{\\dot\\alpha}\\partial_\\mu,\\qquad \\bar Q_{\\dot\\alpha}=-\\partial^L_{\\bar\\theta^{\\dot\\alpha}}+i\\sum_{\\mu,\\alpha}\\theta^\\alpha\\sigma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\dot\\alpha}\\partial_\\mu.$$\n\nFor external odd constant parameters $\\epsilon^\\alpha,\\bar\\epsilon^{\\dot\\alpha}$ that anticommute with the odd generators and coefficient algebra, set $\\delta_{\\epsilon,\\bar\\epsilon}\\Phi=\\sum_\\alpha\\epsilon^\\alpha Q_\\alpha\\Phi+\\sum_{\\dot\\alpha}\\bar\\epsilon^{\\dot\\alpha}\\bar Q_{\\dot\\alpha}\\Phi$. The component transformation is, by definition, the unique ordered-basis coefficient tuple of this transformed superfield in the displayed $y,\\theta$ expansion. The space of fields is classical and smooth, so these differential operations have common domain $\\mathscr S$; no quantum-field operator domain or field equation is assumed. In natural units $[x^\\mu]=-1$ and $[\\theta]=[\\bar\\theta]=-\\tfrac12$. If $[\\Phi]=d$ is assigned, the formal component dimensions are $[A]=d$, $[\\psi_\\alpha]=d+\\tfrac12$, and $[F]=d+1$; no value of $d$ is fixed.",
