          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For each $I,\\alpha$, the supercharge $Q^I_\\alpha$ is a closed densely defined operator on $\\mathcal H$ and $\\bar Q_{\\dot\\alpha I}=(Q^I_\\alpha)^*$ is its actual Hilbert adjoint, with exactly the adjoint domain. The central generators $Z^{IJ}$ and $\\bar Z_{IJ}$ are represented by closed densely defined operators with $\\bar Z_{IJ}=(Z^{IJ})^*$ as an actual Hilbert adjoint. On a common dense graded linear domain $\\mathcal D$, contained in the domains of every generator product in the bracket table, the operator supercommutator is the displayed algebra bracket. In particular,\n\n$$\\{Q^I_\\alpha,\\bar Q_{\\dot\\beta J}\\}=-2\\sigma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\dot\\beta}P_\\mu\\delta^I{}_J,\\qquad \\{Q^I_\\alpha,Q^J_\\beta\\}=2\\epsilon_{\\alpha\\beta}Z^{IJ},\\qquad \\{\\bar Q_{\\dot\\alpha I},\\bar Q_{\\dot\\beta J}\\}=2\\epsilon_{\\dot\\alpha\\dot\\beta}\\bar Z_{IJ}.$$\n\nThe domain $\\mathcal D$ is invariant under those generators and under $U(\\mathcal P)$. The even group acts on the charge domains exactly: for every $g\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)$, conjugation by $U(g,0)$ sends each closed charge to the corresponding fundamental or dotted spinor transform as an equality of operators, including equality of domains. The central generators transform as Lorentz scalars, commute with translations, and commute on $\\mathcal D$ with all supercharges; these relations also include their domain invariance.",
