+
+## Postulate
+
+Use oriented and time-oriented Minkowski spacetime $M=\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with coordinates $x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3$, metric $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and natural units $\hbar=c=1$. Let $n\geq1$, let $U\subseteq\mathbb C^n$ be an open set containing $0$, let $K:U\to\mathbb R$ be smooth, and require the Hermitian matrix $g_{i\bar j}(z,\bar z)=\partial_i\partial_{\bar j}K(z,\bar z)$ to be positive definite at every point of $U$. Let $W:U\to\mathbb C$ be holomorphic. These data specify the Kähler coordinate patch and holomorphic superpotential of the model.
+
+For each $i=1,\ldots,n$, take an even smooth chiral superfield $\Phi^i$ on the four-dimensional superspace, with unique scalar, Weyl-fermion, and complex auxiliary components $(\phi^i,\psi^i_\alpha,F^i)$ as fixed by the chiral-superfield convention. The scalar map $\phi:M\to U$ is smooth with compact support, and the coefficient functions of $\psi^i$ and $F^i$ are smooth and compactly supported. The supersymmetry parameters $\epsilon^\alpha$ and $\bar\epsilon^{\dot\alpha}$ are constant Grassmann-odd Weyl spinors, and the adopted variation is $\delta\Phi^i=(\epsilon^\alpha Q_\alpha+\bar\epsilon^{\dot\alpha}\bar Q_{\dot\alpha})\Phi^i$ with the chiral-superfield supercharges. The engineering dimensions are $[x^\mu]=-1$, $[\theta]=[\bar\theta]=-1/2$, $[\phi^i]=1$, $[\psi^i]=3/2$, $[F^i]=2$, $[K]=2$, and $[W]=3$ in mass units.
+
+Adopt the classical action
+
+$$S_{\mathrm{WZ}}=\int_M d^4x\left(\int d^4\theta\,K(\bar\Phi,\Phi)+\left[\int d^2\theta\,W(\Phi)+\mathrm{h.c.}\right]\right),$$
+
+where the ordered Berezin measures are those fixed for four-dimensional N=1 superspace. In the bosonic zero-derivative sector, the algebraic auxiliary-field terms are
+
+$$\mathcal L_{F}=g_{i\bar j}(\phi,\bar\phi)F^i\bar F^{\bar j}+F^iW_i(\phi)+\bar F^{\bar j}\overline{W_j(\phi)}.$$
+
+The canonical Wess–Zumino model is the special case $U=\mathbb C^n$, $K=\sum_i\bar\Phi^{\bar i}\Phi^i$, and hence $g_{i\bar j}=\delta_{i\bar j}$. This is an adopted classical formulation and normalization, not a deduction that nature realizes the model.
+
+## Postulate references
+
+[Stephen P. Martin, *A Supersymmetry Primer*, §§3.1–3.2 and 4.1–4.7](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-ph/9709356v7).
+
+[Adel Bilal, *Introduction to Supersymmetry*, §§4.2–4.3 and 7.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0101055).
+
