+
+## Statement
+
+For the adopted rigid N=1 SU(2) gauge-matter superspace action, use the full real vector prepotential $V$ and the chiral matter superfields $\Phi^a$ without fixing Wess–Zumino gauge. Assume smooth compactly supported vector and matter component fluctuations in the local trivializing regions used for the variation. Under the global supertranslation $\delta=(\epsilon^\alpha Q_\alpha+\bar\epsilon^{\dot\alpha}\bar Q_{\dot\alpha})$ with constant Grassmann-odd parameters, the variation of every full-superspace D-term is a sum of odd-coordinate derivatives and a spacetime divergence. The same is true of the chiral F-terms built from the gauge-invariant holomorphic superpotential and the chiral vector field strength. Compact support removes the spacetime boundary contributions, so the complete action is invariant. This is a conditional classical invariance statement for the full superspace fields; it does not assert that Wess–Zumino gauge is preserved by an uncompensated supersymmetry transformation.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[Stephen P. Martin, *A Supersymmetry Primer*, §§4.2 and 4.8–4.9](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-ph/9709356v7).
+
+[Adel Bilal, *Introduction to Supersymmetry*, §§4.3 and 5.1–5.2](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0101055).
+
+[[post-qftx-n1-gauge-matter-model]]
+
+[[def-qftx-n1-vector-superfield]]
+
+[[def-qftx-n1-chiral-superfield]]
+
+[[lem-qftx-n1-chiral-superspace-identities]]
+
+[[def-qftx-superspace-grassmann-algebra]]
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The adopted gauge-matter action, full vector prepotential, and compactly supported matter fluctuations in the Statement.
+
+[A1] [[post-qftx-n1-gauge-matter-model]]: the action is a finite sum of full-superspace terms $[\bar\Phi^a e^{2gV_{R_a}}\Phi^a]_D$ and chiral terms $[\frac{1}{4g^2}\kappa_\mathbb C(\mathcal W^\alpha,\mathcal W_\alpha)+W_G(\Phi)]_F+\mathrm{h.c.}$, $W_G$ is gauge invariant, and the field-dependent component fluctuations in the local trivializing regions used for the variation are smooth and compactly supported.
+
+[F1] [[def-qftx-n1-chiral-superfield]]: $Q_\alpha$ and $\bar Q_{\dot\alpha}$ are the fixed odd first-order superspace differential operators, and chiral superfields have the unique shifted-coordinate expansion.
+
+[F2] [[def-qftx-n1-vector-superfield]]: $V$ is a smooth even real matrix-valued superfield and $\mathcal W_\alpha$ is a chiral field-strength superfield.
+
+[F3] [[lem-qftx-n1-chiral-superspace-identities]]: the supercharges preserve the chiral constraint and chiral fields are independent of $\bar\theta$ in $y=x+i\theta\sigma\bar\theta$ coordinates.
+
+[F4] [[def-qftx-superspace-grassmann-algebra]]: left Grassmann derivatives are odd derivations and ordered Berezin integration extracts the top coefficient of the finite four-generator algebra.
+
+## Proof
+
+**Given:** [A1] and the superspace data and boundary convention in [F1]–[F4].
+
+1.1 For an even smooth matrix-valued superspace integrand $X$, expand $\delta X$ using [F1]. The odd Leibniz rule and constancy of the parameters give $\delta X=-\partial^L_{\theta^\alpha}(\epsilon^\alpha X)+\partial^L_{\bar\theta^{\dot\alpha}}(\bar\epsilon^{\dot\alpha}X)+\partial_\mu(-i\epsilon^\alpha\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\beta}\bar\theta^{\dot\beta}X+i\bar\epsilon^{\dot\alpha}\theta^\beta\sigma^\mu_{\beta\dot\alpha}X)$. The same identity holds entrywise for matrices because $Q$ acts by derivations. [F1, F4, algebra]
+
+1.2 For $X=\sum_a\bar\Phi^a e^{2gV_{R_a}}\Phi^a$, the finite Grassmann expansion has smooth coefficients, and the ordinary finite-dimensional matrix exponential is smooth in the body coefficients; hence step 1.1 applies. The odd-coordinate derivatives have no top Grassmann coefficient by [F4], so their D-term integrals vanish. The spacetime divergence integrates to zero under the compact-support or relative-action boundary condition in [A1]. Thus every matter D-term is invariant. [A1, F2, F4, step 1.1]
+
+2.1 The superpotential is chiral: the chain rule gives $\bar D_{\dot\alpha}W_G(\Phi)=\sum_a(\partial W_G/\partial\Phi^a)\bar D_{\dot\alpha}\Phi^a=0$. The vector field strength is chiral by [F2]. Therefore both $W_G(\Phi)$ and $\kappa_\mathbb C(\mathcal W^\alpha,\mathcal W_\alpha)$ are chiral even superfields. [A1, F2, F3, algebra]
+
+2.2 For either chiral integrand $Y$ in step 2.1, change to $y=x+i\theta\sigma\bar\theta$. By [F1] and [F3], $Q_\alpha=\partial^L_{\theta^\alpha}|_y$ and $\bar Q_{\dot\alpha}=-\partial^L_{\bar\theta^{\dot\alpha}}|_y+2i\theta^\beta\sigma^\mu_{\beta\dot\alpha}\partial_{y^\mu}$. Since $Y$ is independent of $\bar\theta$, $\delta Y=\epsilon^\alpha\partial^L_{\theta^\alpha}Y+2i\bar\epsilon^{\dot\alpha}\theta^\beta\sigma^\mu_{\beta\dot\alpha}\partial_\mu Y$. The first term integrates to zero by Berezin coefficient extraction, and the second is a spacetime divergence with vanishing boundary contribution. The conjugate antichiral terms obey the conjugate identity. [A1, F1, F3, F4, step 2.1]
+
+3.1 The D-term and all chiral F-term variations in steps 1.2 and 2.2 integrate to zero. Their sum is the complete action in [A1], so $\delta S_{\mathrm{GM}}=0$. The argument uses only the declared superspace variation and auxiliary fields; it invokes no equations of motion, gauge fixing, or quantum construction. [A1, step 1.2, step 2.2] ∎
